title: Language Overview
files:
  - em.core:em.utils/SoftUart.em.ts
  - em.core:em.mcu/Common.em.ts
  - rpi.2040:rpi.distro.2040/BoardC.em.ts
  - em.core:em.utils/LedT.em.ts
  - .assets/img-100-03-01.png
  - em.prog/em.examples.basic/Ex02_BlinkerP.em.ts

steps:
  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;EM&bull;Script Language Overview}

      If not already obvious, **EM&bull;Script** ≡ **TypeScript**&thinsp;!!

      Using *progrmming conventions* reinenforced visually by **EM&bull;Browser**, **TypeScript** effectively "hosts" **EM&bull;Script**.

      But why **TypeScript** [**TS**] &ndash; especially when targeting embed&shy;ded HW typically programmed in C&thinsp;??

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} start with a stable, widely-deployed language&thinsp;....<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} leverage its robust support within **VS Code**&thinsp;....<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} add a novel back-end program translator&thinsp;....<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} generate highly-optimized target C/C++&thinsp;....

      This tour introduces some core constructs of **EM&bull;Script** [**EMS**] &ndash; but viewed through a **TS** language lens.

  - cmds:
      - mark 1 M 13
      - mark 1 T 19
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,splitscreen]&thinsp;One Unit &ndash; Two Domains}

      The {[cx]em.utils/SoftUart.em.ts} **Unit** implements a low-level (albeit portable) *bit-banged* serial transmitter.

      When running on target HW, the {[cf]put} function serializes a single byte of data over some physical GPIO pin.

      If used by an **EM&bull;Script** application, however, this **Unit** executes within <i><u>two</u></i> distinct programming domains:

      {[bi,counter_1]} *target* domain, where functions like {[cf]put} will run<br>
      {[bi,counter_2]} *meta* domain, where app configuration takes place

      Unlike resource-limited domain {[bi,counter_1]}, code belonging to domain {[bi,counter_2]} runs inside the **EMS** translator &ndash; on your PC.

      The **TS** namespace at {[bm,M]} contains all of the *meta* code; the special comment at {[bm,T]} begins the *target* code.

  - cmds:
      - mark 1 M 13
      - mark 1 T 19
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,timeline]&thinsp;Lifecycle Functions}

      The {[cu]SoftUart} **Unit** implements two *intrinsic* functions called during the **EM&bull;Script** program lifecycle:

      {[cs]em$construct} [**`meta`**]&thinsp; called to initialize private state<br>
      {[cs]em$startup&nbsp;&nbsp;} [**`targ`**]&thinsp; called before program {[cs]em$run}

      {[in]Programs like %[cu]BlinkerP% viewed in earlier tours imple&shy;ment the %[cs]em$run% intrinsic as their main entry-point.}

      The **EMS** translator sequences calls to intrinsic func&shy;tions &ndash; at build-time [**`meta`**] as well as run-time [**`targ`**].

      By design, each intrinsic belongs to only *one* **EMS** do&shy;main &ndash; and may apply to only some **EMS Unit** *flavors*.

  - cmds:
      - mark 1 I 5
      - mark 1 E 26
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,handshake]&thinsp;Client&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;Supplier Relationship}

      The {[ck]import} directives in {[cu]SoftUart} establish a *client&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;sup&shy;plier* relationship between this and other **Units**.

      The {[ck]export} qualifiers in {[cu]SoftUart} make functions like {[cf]flush} and {[cf]put} publically visible to clients of this **Unit**.

      All **Units** so far have played the **Module** role &ndash; hiding its *private* implementation of its *public* specification.

      In the case of {[cu]SoftUart}, some of its public spec in fact derives from {[cu]ConsoleUartI} &ndash; an **EMS Interface**.

      {[in]Select %[cu]ConsoleUartI% on line %[bm,I]% and press <b>F12</b> to see the original "spec-only" definition of %[cf]flush% and %[cf]put%.}

      Each **EMS Interface** in general defines a *programmatic contract* between **EMS Module** clients and suppliers.

  - cmds:
      - mark 1 C 8
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,settings]&thinsp;Configuration Parameters}

      Line {[bm,C]} exports an intrinsic **EMS** {[cs]$config} parameter named {[cn]baud_rate} &ndash; which clients can assign as needed.

      This {[cs]$config} parameter holds a 32-bit unsigned integer [{[ct]u32}] with a default value of {[ce]57_600}.

      Most critical, {[cs]$config} assignments *only* occur at build-time [**`meta`**].&thinsp; At run-time their values remain constant.

      {[cu]SoftUart} also declares {[cn]bit_time} &ndash; a private {[cs]$config} bound by the intrinsic {[cs]em$construct} [**`meta`**] function.

      Run-time function like {[cf]put} will then use {[cn]bit_time} no differently from any statically initialized constant.

      By contrast, legacy UART drivers often execute *run-time* C code to set a baud-rate likely known at *build-time*.

  - cmds:
      - mark 1 P 9

    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,plug_connect]&thinsp;Client&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;Supplier Proxies}

      Line {[bm,P]} exports a {[cn]TxPin} {[cs]$proxy} &ndash; which {[cu]SoftUart} clients must bind to a conformant *delegate* **Module**.

      Like {[cs]$config} values, a {[cs]$proxy} declares the *type* of its delegate &ndash; specified through an *abstract* **Interface**.

      As such, each {[cs]$proxy} serves as a place&shy;holder for some *concrete* **Module** &ndash; but from an unknown supplier.

      {[in]Select %[cu]GpioI% at its %[ck]import% statement and press <bf>F12</b> to see the functions callable through %[cs]$proxy% %[cn]TxPin%.}

      At run-time [**`targ`**], calls to {[cn]TxPin}**.**{[cf]set} behave as if {[cu]SoftUart} had directly imported the delegate **Module**.

      But thanks to the **EMS** translator, these apparent in&shy;directions "melt away" to (inlined) functions calls.

  - cmds:
      - mark 1 A 30
      - mark 1 B 32
      - mark 1 C 33
      - mark 1 D 39
    focus: [1, 30]
    text: |

      {[ht,electric_bolt]&thinsp;Bit-Banging Those Bytes}

      Let's dive into the implementation of {[cf]put}, which trans&shy;mits 8-bit [{[ct]u8}] {[cn]data} as a framed 10-bit sequence.

      {[BM,A]} {[cn]tx_bits} contains the frame's leading START bits<br>
      {[BM,B]} &thinsp;a **TS**-style {[ck]for} loop iterating from {[ce]0}..{[cn]bit_cnt}<br>
      {[BM,C]} {[cf]set}&middot;{[cf]clear} the {[cn]TxPin} GPIO based on {[cn]tx_bits}<br>
      {[BM,D]} &thinsp;shift {[cn]tx_bits} and {[cf]spin} for {[cn]bit_time} &mu;secs

      {[in]Stay seated &ndash; we'll visit %[cu]Common% at our next stop&thinsp;!!}

      Remember, **EMS** ≡ **TS** &ndash; some new keywords, familiar C-like stmts &middot; exprs, and *optional*&thinsp; trailing semi-colons.

      Compared with C, **EMS** offers a higher-level program&shy;ming paradigm *AND*&thinsp; a higher-level of performance.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,hub]&thinsp;Pluggable Software Backplane}

      The rather sparse {[cu]Common} **Module** shown here takes the **EMS** {[cs]$proxy} pattern to an entirely new level.

      Used widely in the codebase, {[cu]Common} en&shy;ables its clients to *portably* invoke "low-level" hardware functions.

      Unlike a legacy HAL [HW abstraction layer], {[cu]Common} acts more like a mix-and-match, *pluggable* SW backplane.

      Despite this extra level of indirection, calls through a {[cu]Common} {[cs]$proxy} incur no additional run-time overhead.

      {[in]Use <b>F12</b> to view the <b>Interface</b> spec of each %[cs]$proxy%.}

      And now, let's see how {[cs]$proxy} (as well as {[cs]$config}) bindings often occur at program build-time [**`meta`**].

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - mark 3 A 8
      - mark 3 B 29
      - mark 3 C 68
    focus: [3, 1]
    acts: ["$reveal"]
    text: |

      {[ht,api]&thinsp;Integration &middot; Specialization}

      We'll now introduce a new flavor of **EMS Unit** &ndash; the **Composite**, as exemplified by {[cu]BoardC}.

      A **Composite** will generally *wire-up &middot; fine-tune* other program ele&shy;ments through {[cs]$proxy}&middot;{[cs]$config} bindings.

      Unlike other **Units** we've seen, {[cu]BoardC} resides in a *HW-specific* **Package** supporting the **Raspberry Pi Pico**.

      {[in]The <span class="em codicon codicon-circuit-board"></span> status item shows the <i>current</i> target board.}

      {[BM,A]}{[cu]BoardC} imports portable & non-portable **Modules**<br>
      {[BM,B]}{[cu]BoardC} creates *new* **Modules** using {[cs]$clone} [TBD]<br>
      {[BM,C]}{[cu]BoardC} binds {[cs]$proxy} delegates and {[cs]$config} values

      The {[cs]em$configure} [**`meta`**] intrinsic often serves as the focal point within this (and other) {[cu]BoardC} **Composites**.

  - cmds:
      - open 4
      - mark 4 I 5
      - mark 4 T 9
      - mark 4 M 11
      - mark 4 C 65
    focus: [4, 1]
    acts: ["$reveal"]
    text: |

      {[ht,control_point_duplicate]&thinsp;Templates for Cloning}

      We've now come to our last flavor of **EMS Unit** &ndash; the **Template**, as exemplified by {[cu]LedT}.

      {[BM,T]} **Templates** feature the **`em$template`** namespace<br>
      {[BM,M]} **`em$template`** envelopes a **Module** prototype<br>
      {[BM,I]} this prototype often implements an **Interface**<br>
      {[BM,C]} **Composites** typically invoke its {[cs]$clone} intrinsic

      {[in]Go <b>Back</b> one stop to review use of <code class="cmd-cs">$clone</code> in <code class="cmd-cu">BoardC</code>.}

      **Templates** (like **Composites**) only exist at *build-time* on your PC &ndash; instantiated very early by the **EMS** translator.

      Clones of the **Module** prototype, of course, will have a presence at build-time [**`meta`**] *and* run-time [**`targ`**]. 

      {[in] Try clicking <b>New Template</b> on your <code>zgit.*</code> <b>Bucket</b>. }

  - cmds:
      - view 5
    text: |

      {[ht,schema]&thinsp;Language Grammar}

      The **EM&bull;porium** includes a complete gram&shy;mar for the **EMS** language &ndash; accessed via the **EM&bull;Home** editor tab.

      Once inside, you'll see all **EMS** language constructs pre&shy;sented through a network of *railroad-track* diagrams.

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} green rectangle {[em]⇒} (non-terminal) syntactic category<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} beige rounded rect {[em]⇒} (terminal) lexical category<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} literal text {[em]⇒} &laquo;{[ck]keyword}&raquo;, &laquo;{[cs]intrinsic}&raquo;, etc

      Clicking on non-terminals such as **decl** or **expr** navigate to their point of definition within the grammar.

      Use the **Back** &middot; **Top** &middot; **Next** buttons at the top-left corner for navigating in multiple directions.

      {[in]Use the <b>Top</b> diagram as a high-level grammar index.}

  - cmds:
      - open 6
    focus: [6, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      We've now come full circle back to {[cu]BlinkerP} &ndash; and with more knowledge of its EMS underpinnings.

      The next group of **Basic Examples** tours in fact presents a dozen different variations of our {[cu]BlinkerP} theme.

      Before proceeding onward, however, reinforce what you've already absorbed about the **EMS** language:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} use **VS Code Intellisense** to explore `.em.ts` files<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} descend into each **CATEGORY** within its grammar<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} use **Search** to find examples of some &laquo;{[cs]intrinsic}&raquo;<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} expand **EM&bull;Script Output** and relish its translator


      {[hc]}
